def max_distinct_window_sum(nums, w):
    if w <= 0 or len(nums) < w:
        return -1

    best = -1
    window_sum = 0
    counts = {}
    left = 0

    for right in range(len(nums)):
        window_sum += nums[right]
        counts[nums[right]] = counts.get(nums[right], 0) + 1

        if right - left + 1 > w:
            window_sum -= nums[left]
            counts[nums[left]] -= 1
            if counts[nums[left]] == 0:
                del counts[nums[left]]
            left += 1

        if right - left + 1 == w and len(counts) == w:
            best = max(best, window_sum)

    return best
```

The sliding window keeps a running sum and a frequency map, so each position is added and removed once. A window qualifies when the map holds exactly w distinct keys, and the best qualifying sum is tracked. Time is O(n) and space is O(w).
